{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST be the home of deriving an initial requirement set from existing code.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The only clause is: \"the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST be the home of deriving an initial requirement set from existing code.\" Under the parent `requirements.cli.skill_structure`, a skill is its `SKILL.md` plus its `references/` files. A topic's home is the one skill that states it in full.\n\nThis clause is about where guidance lives. It prescribes no library, algorithm, signature, type, field or delegation. The one thing it fixes is which skill holds the topic. The supplied root is `assets/skills/habeas-bootstrap-requirements/SKILL.md` (whole file), which is that skill's entry point. A skill that held the topic elsewhere would not meet the clause, and moving the guidance into this skill is the only way to meet it.\n\nThe root's content fits the clause, but the tool output elided most of the body text. The frontmatter says it is for adopting Habeas in an existing codebase. It lists inventorying tests, Rust items and user-visible behavior, and proposing an initial requirement set for approval. It also covers tagging and driving `habeas author`. The `SKILL.md` has sections for Workflow, Inventories, Proposing requirements, Tagging, Report and Gotchas. The description sends missing test references and stale evidence to `habeas-evidence`, so those topics are not duplicated here.\n\nThe requirement has an implementation edge to this root, and no other implementation root is needed. I did not check whether other skills duplicate the topic. That is a content check against the parent requirement, not a structural obligation of this root.\n\nbehavioral: The subject has two observable obligations. The `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill must state the topic of deriving an initial requirement set from existing code. Under the parent `requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the other bundled skills must not state that topic, so the bootstrap skill is its only home. `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_bootstrap_skill_is_home_of_deriving_an_initial_requirement_set]` covers both.\n\n- **Home states the topic.** The test reads the bootstrap `SKILL.md`. It requires the sections `## Inventories`, `## Proposing requirements`, `## Tagging` and `## Report`. It then asserts distinctive phrases in the proposing, tagging and report sections. Examples are \"Write one requirement per obligation a user could rely on\", \"`{test}` on tests and `{impl}` on roots\", and the report fields `Tests mapped:`, `Items mapped:`, `Independent recount:` and `Gaps:`.\n- **No other skill restates it.** `assert_home` in the same file runs `assert_phrases` on the home skill using `home_markers`. For each of the other four skills in `SKILLS`, it asserts that the skill's text contains none of the bootstrap markers. Those markers are \"every first-party runnable test or test case\", \"## Proposing requirements\" and \"Independent recount:\". Together they cover the inventory, proposing and report parts of the topic.\n\nThe tests assert real outcomes, and a skill that dropped the bootstrap content or duplicated these parts elsewhere would fail them. Spot-check markers are the project's chosen way to detect \"stated in full\", and the requirement text sets no finer boundary. I see no obligation that is untested or ambiguous.",
