      "output": {
        "status": "success",
        "rationale": "The subject has two observable obligations. The `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill must state the topic of deriving an initial requirement set from existing code. Under the parent `requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the other bundled skills must not state that topic, so the bootstrap skill is its only home. `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_bootstrap_skill_is_home_of_deriving_an_initial_requirement_set]` covers both.\n\n- **Home states the topic.** The test reads the bootstrap `SKILL.md`. It requires the sections `## Inventories`, `## Proposing requirements`, `## Tagging` and `## Report`. It then asserts distinctive phrases in the proposing, tagging and report sections. Examples are \"Write one requirement per obligation a user could rely on\", \"`{test}` on tests and `{impl}` on roots\", and the report fields `Tests mapped:`, `Items mapped:`, `Independent recount:` and `Gaps:`.\n- **No other skill restates it.** `assert_home` in the same file runs `assert_phrases` on the home skill using `home_markers`. For each of the other four skills in `SKILLS`, it asserts that the skill's text contains none of the bootstrap markers. Those markers are \"every first-party runnable test or test case\", \"## Proposing requirements\" and \"Independent recount:\". Together they cover the inventory, proposing and report parts of the topic.\n\nThe tests assert real outcomes, and a skill that dropped the bootstrap content or duplicated these parts elsewhere would fail them. Spot-check markers are the project's chosen way to detect \"stated in full\", and the requirement text sets no finer boundary. I see no obligation that is untested or ambiguous."
      }
